    "q-117217": {
      "dom_id": "step-q117217",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14240607/assets/afc68dbb5c336cff31e75f5ed92dd853c8fc7ec43e8fa66539d27d83b9e2070f.png)\n\nA box with no lid and a square base has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$3$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$5$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $2\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ for the box that has the smallest production cost?",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =3{x}^{2}+5\\cdot 4xy \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+20xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $2\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 2 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =3{x}^{2}+20xy \\\\ C & =3{x}^{2}+20x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ C & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{40}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=6x-\\frac{40}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 6x-\\frac{40}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3} & =40 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=6+\\frac{80}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}})=6+12=18>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{20}{3}}$ is indeed a minimum of $C(x).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
