    "q-347083": {
      "dom_id": "step-q347083",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14240607/assets/d67ba70d92807f0a7298fb8222252a047f7eec03a26878a5aa95b0fb82a7335c.png)\n\nA box with no lid whose depth $z$ is $3$ times its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$2$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $972\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2xz+1\\cdot 2xy+1\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =2xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $972\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 972 & =x\\cdot z\\cdot (\\frac{z}{3}) \\\\ 972 & =\\frac{1}{3}x{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x{z}^{2} & =972 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2\\,(\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}})\\,z+2\\,(\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}})\\,(\\frac{z}{3})+2\\,(\\frac{z}{3})\\,z \\\\  & =\\frac{5832}{z}+\\frac{1944}{z}+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7776}{z}+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=\\frac{4}{3}z-\\frac{7776}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}z-\\frac{7776}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{z}^{3} & =7776 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =5832 \\\\ z & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=18$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{15552}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(18)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=4>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(18)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=18$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=18$ units.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
