      "dom_id": "step-q347273",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14240607/assets/12a9961800e0578de92b428e1a1a6ba5e10e25fe635a90f85c772f9bf73dc997.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $15\\,cm\\times 7\\,cm.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{cm}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height $y$ of the box that gives the maximum possible volume.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=15-2y,\\,z=7-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $15-2y>0$ and $7-2y>0$, hence $y<\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(15-2y)(y)(7-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-44{y}^{2}+105y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-88y+105,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-88y+105 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{35}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $\\frac{35}{6}$ since we already know that $0<y<\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{3}{2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-88.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{3}{2}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the height of the box with the maximum volume is $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\text{ cm}.$",
